                <head>427. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On February 21, 1968, President Johnson and UN Secretary General U Thant met for
                    a discussion of the Secretary General’s most recent effort to find a solution to
                    the Vietnam war. U Thant reported that the North Vietnamese were willing to
                    begin talks “immediately” upon the cessation of U.S. bombing raids on their
                    territory. The Secretary General urged the President to undertake another
                    bombing pause to test the sincerity of the North Vietnamese. The President
                    expressed his doubts about North Vietnam’s objectives but encouraged U Thant’s
                    efforts. A tape recording of their conversation is in the Johnson Library,
                    Recordings and Transcripts of Cabinet Room Meetings, Tape C002A–1D. A 3-page
                    summary of the meeting and an unedited transcript are ibid. Johnson discussed
                    the meeting in The Vantage Point, page 395.</p>
            </div>
